\DOC LOCAL_ADD_4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_ADD_4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Steps an {eventually}-style machine-code goal forward across one inlined
modular-addition subroutine, advancing the symbolic state variable and adding
assumptions describing the new state and the result stored in memory.

\DESCRIBE
{LOCAL_ADD_4_TAC} takes an {int} offset, a positional/offset parameter of the
step, and a {string list} of three names: the first names the result variable
whose value is asserted to be stored in memory, and the other two name the
operand variables, the summands appearing in the added arithmetic assumption.
Applied to a goal whose conclusion is an {eventually} claim about a symbolic
machine state, it produces exactly one subgoal. In the conclusion, the state
term the {eventually} predicate is applied to becomes the next symbolic state
variable, the predicate body being unchanged. Every assumption mentioning the
old state variable — the program-counter assumption, the {bytes_loaded}
assumption, the register and memory {read} assumptions, and the accumulated
{MAYCHANGE} assumption relating the initial state to the current one — is
discharged and re-added with the new state variable, the {MAYCHANGE} assumption
gaining one further component for the memory block just written. Two
assumptions are added: a new memory {read} assumption giving the stored result
value, and an implication bounding the two operands below the modulus and
concluding that the result equals their sum. Assumptions that do not mention
the state variable (nonoverlapping conditions, earlier arithmetic facts, side
conditions) are kept unchanged, though their position in the list may shift.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always on
goals whose conclusion is an {eventually} claim about a symbolic machine state.

\ENDDOC
